(6904762, 1037756, 112739, '<td valign="top">A location well worth the effort of the hike. So many elements make this an extraordinary image. Congrats on HM. (a little late on commenting)</td>', 'A location well worth the effort of the hike. So many elements make this an extraordinary image. Congrats on HM. (a little late on commenting)', '2012-11-12 17:39:26.000000', NULL, 0),
(6904766, 1039794, 112739, '<td valign="top">Is the relative lack of star movement with a 76 sec exposure due to your northern location? more north, less movement? Seems unfair somehow. (: Congrats on HM! Would love to come up and try this sometime.</td>', 'Is the relative lack of star movement with a 76 sec exposure due to your northern location? more north, less movement? Seems unfair somehow. (: Congrats on HM! Would love to come up and try this sometime.', '2012-11-12 17:43:52.000000', NULL, 0),
(6904769, 1039098, 112739, '<td valign="top">I thought this was one of the most original shots this month. Love the motion blur and composition. Congrats on TT.</td>', 'I thought this was one of the most original shots this month. Love the motion blur and composition. Congrats on TT.', '2012-11-12 17:45:16.000000', NULL, 0),
(6905092, 1039794, 35720, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by Dennisheckman:</b></div><hr/><i> Is the relative lack of star movement with a 76 sec exposure due to your northern location? more north, less movement? Seems unfair somehow. (: Congrats on HM! Would love to come up and try this sometime. </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>No there is another explanation: The lack of movement is because the exposure on the sky was less than 30 sec, after that time I covered the upper part of the lens so there was a 76 sec exposure just on the lower part. You have to try this sometime. Thanks a lot for your comment Dennis ;-)</td>', 'Originally posted by Dennisheckman: Is the relative lack of star movement with a 76 sec exposure due to your northern location? more north, less movement? Seems unfair somehow. (: Congrats on HM! Would love to come up and try this sometime.   No there is another explanation: The lack of movement is because the exposure on the sky was less than 30 sec, after that time I covered the upper part of the lens so there was a 76 sec exposure just on the lower part. You have to try this sometime. Thanks a lot for your comment Dennis ;-)', '2012-11-13 09:00:21.000000', NULL, 0),
(6906065, 1039393, 19825, '<td valign="top">Congrats on the ton of PH bling you received on this fascinating image.</td>', 'Congrats on the ton of PH bling you received on this fascinating image.', '2012-11-14 11:39:00.000000', NULL, 0),
(6906119, 1038784, 72929, '<td valign="top">FYI, those minute+ long "shooting stars" are really satellites, and they fade to darkness as they enter Earth''s shadow (especially the ones in polar orbits). In your outtakes, "Sawtooth venus meteor" looks like a satellite (although it may not be).<br/> <br/>In years of observing, shooting stars are very brief, 1-2 seconds max, and have ''tails''- growing brighter, then dimmer. Sometimes they have color (green, pink, orange, yellow).<br/> <br/>That said, your image here is lovely. The clouds add to the result.</td>', 'FYI, those minute+ long "shooting stars" are really satellites, and they fade to darkness as they enter Earth''s shadow (especially the ones in polar orbits). In your outtakes, "Sawtooth venus meteor" looks like a satellite (although it may not be). In years of observing, shooting stars are very brief, 1-2 seconds max, and have ''tails''- growing brighter, then dimmer. Sometimes they have color (green, pink, orange, yellow). That said, your image here is lovely. The clouds add to the result.', '2012-11-14 12:27:43.000000', NULL, 0),
(6906181, 1038668, 74559, '<td valign="top">This image is truly stunning, I absolutely love it! Congrats on your ribbon, and congrats for winning JPEG''s <a href="//jpgmag.com/photos/3458136/?utm_source=newsletter&amp;utm_medium=email&amp;utm_campaign=newsletter20121111" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">Photo of the Week</a> as well!!</td>', 'This image is truly stunning, I absolutely love it! Congrats on your ribbon, and congrats for winning JPEG''s Photo of the Week as well!!', '2012-11-14 13:46:32.000000', NULL, 0),
(6906200, 1038668, 96574, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by cgino:</b></div><hr/><i> This image is truly stunning, I absolutely love it! Congrats on your ribbon, and congrats for winning JPEG''s <a href="//jpgmag.com/photos/3458136/?utm_source=newsletter&amp;utm_medium=email&amp;utm_campaign=newsletter20121111" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">Photo of the Week</a> as well!! </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>Thanks Colleen! :)<br/>And thanks to everyone for all the really great comments.</td>', 'Originally posted by cgino: This image is truly stunning, I absolutely love it! Congrats on your ribbon, and congrats for winning JPEG''s Photo of the Week as well!!   Thanks Colleen! :)And thanks to everyone for all the really great comments.', '2012-11-14 14:24:27.000000', NULL, 0),
